# Argus configuration.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and edit:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Compose reads `.env` automatically and `./run.sh` passes it through.
#
# Your GitHub token does NOT belong in here. It is injected at run time
# from 1Password or from your `gh` login, so it never touches disk - see
# op.env.example and the token setup section of the README.


# ======================================================================
#  Required
# ======================================================================

# The GitHub organisation to sweep. Argus refuses to start without it
# rather than guessing.
ARGUS_GITHUB_ORG=your-org-here


# ======================================================================
#  Which repositories
# ======================================================================
#
# By default Argus sweeps the whole organisation, minus archived
# repositories. These apply everywhere - pull requests, branches and
# security alerts alike - so a repository left out here is absent from
# all of them rather than from just one.

# An allowlist. Set it and only these are swept; leave it unset for the
# whole organisation. Names only, no owner prefix, comma separated.
# A long list is truncated to keep the search query inside GitHub's
# limit, so prefer ARGUS_EXCLUDE_REPOS if you have many repositories.
# ARGUS_REPOS=api,web,infra

# Repositories to leave out. Ignored when ARGUS_REPOS is set, since an
# allowlist already says what is included.
# ARGUS_EXCLUDE_REPOS=sandbox,playground

# Archived repositories are skipped by default - nothing in an archive is
# actionable. Set this to sweep them anyway.
# ARGUS_INCLUDE_ARCHIVED=false


# ======================================================================
#  Server
# ======================================================================

# Deliberately unusual, so it will not collide with a dev server on
# 3000 / 5173 / 8000 / 8080. Leave the container running and it stays warm.
#
# Whatever you pick, http://argus.localhost:<port> works with no setup at
# all - browsers and modern operating systems resolve *.localhost to
# 127.0.0.1 without an /etc/hosts entry.
#
# Setting this to 80 gets you a bare http://argus.localhost with no port,
# which is the prettiest option. The trade is that port 80 is a shared
# resource: anything else wanting it later will conflict.
# ARGUS_PORT=18474

# How often the background sweep runs. This is what makes the dashboard
# instant: you read the last result from memory instead of waiting on a
# fresh sweep. Lower is fresher but spends more API quota.
# ARGUS_REFRESH_MINUTES=15

# Maximum GitHub requests in flight at once, across every rule. Raising
# this speeds up large organisations but risks GitHub's secondary rate
# limits.
# ARGUS_CONCURRENCY=10

# Per-request timeout, in seconds.
# ARGUS_HTTP_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=45

# The interface to listen on. Loopback by default, and you should leave
# it that way: the dashboard has no authentication, so anything that can
# reach it can read your organisation's data through your token. The
# container sets this to 0.0.0.0 internally, and Compose publishes the
# port to 127.0.0.1 only, which is what actually limits exposure.
# ARGUS_BIND=127.0.0.1

# Only change this if something else on your machine is already called
# argus.
# ARGUS_CONTAINER_NAME=argus


# ======================================================================
#  Jira — only needed for the sprint report
# ======================================================================
#
# Leave these unset and the sprint tool stays hidden; the pull request
# tool needs none of them.
#
# Nothing here has a default that assumes a particular company, and field
# ids deliberately do not: customfield_10016 is Story Points on one site
# and something else entirely on the next, so they are resolved by name
# unless you pin them.

# ARGUS_JIRA_BASE_URL=https://yourcompany.atlassian.net
# ARGUS_JIRA_EMAIL=you@yourcompany.com
# ARGUS_JIRA_PROJECT=ABC

# The token goes in op.env, not here - see op.env.example.
# Create one at id.atlassian.com/manage-profile/security/api-tokens

# Status names that count as delivered. Matched by name rather than by
# Jira's "done" category, because teams usually have several statuses in
# that category and disagree about which of them mean finished.
# ARGUS_JIRA_DONE_STATUSES=Done

# Issue types left out of the say/do count - normally the container types
# that carry no commitment of their own.
# ARGUS_JIRA_EXCLUDED_TYPES=Epic

# Nominal sprint length in working days, used to turn days off into a
# share of someone's baseline.
# ARGUS_JIRA_SPRINT_LENGTH_DAYS=10

# Pin a custom field id if resolving it by name picks the wrong one.
# ARGUS_JIRA_POINTS_FIELD=customfield_XXXXX
# ARGUS_JIRA_POINTS_FIELD_NAME=Story Points
# ARGUS_JIRA_SPRINT_FIELD=customfield_XXXXX
# ARGUS_JIRA_SPRINT_FIELD_NAME=Sprint

# Every write the sprint tool can make is gated behind this, and it is
# off by default: a tool that can reassign tickets and publish pages
# should be something you switch on deliberately.
# ARGUS_SPRINT_ALLOW_WRITES=false


# ======================================================================
#  Rules
# ======================================================================
#
# Every check is independent. Turn one off, or retune it, without
# touching code - uncomment a line to override its default, then
# `./run.sh restart`.
#
# The Rules panel in the dashboard lists all of these live, including any
# added after this file was written.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Open pull requests  [merge_readiness]
#
# Every open, review-ready pull request, with its review decision and
# check status attached.
# The full inventory. The status column is the point: it separates what is
# approved and green from what is still waiting on a review or a fix.
# ARGUS_RULE_MERGE_READINESS_ENABLED=true
#
# Skip draft pull requests.
# ARGUS_RULE_MERGE_READINESS_EXCLUDE_DRAFTS=true
#
# Authors to ignore entirely, comma separated.
# ARGUS_RULE_MERGE_READINESS_EXCLUDE_AUTHORS=app/dependabot
#
# A label that gates merge in your workflow, if you use one. Leave empty
# to disable the check.
# ARGUS_RULE_MERGE_READINESS_QA_LABEL=
#
# Check names that are policy gates rather than real CI, comma separated.
# Matching failures are reported separately so they do not read as broken
# builds.
# ARGUS_RULE_MERGE_READINESS_IGNORE_CHECKS=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Your open pull requests  [my_prs]
#
# Every open pull request you authored, drafts included. The opposite of
# the review queue, which holds other people's work waiting on you.
# Drafts are deliberately included here. A draft you opened and forgot is
# invisible everywhere else, and that is exactly how work goes stale.
# ARGUS_RULE_MY_PRS_ENABLED=true
#
# A label that gates merge in your workflow, if you use one. Leave empty
# to disable the check.
# ARGUS_RULE_MY_PRS_QA_LABEL=
#
# Check names that are policy gates rather than real CI, comma separated.
# Matching failures are reported separately so they do not read as broken
# builds.
# ARGUS_RULE_MY_PRS_IGNORE_CHECKS=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Waiting on your review  [review_requested]
#
# Other people's open pull requests where a review is requested from you,
# or from a team you belong to. Not your own work - that is listed
# separately.
# These are blocked on you specifically. Nothing else on the dashboard is
# as directly your move.
# ARGUS_RULE_REVIEW_REQUESTED_ENABLED=true
#
# Also count reviews requested from your teams, not just you personally.
# ARGUS_RULE_REVIEW_REQUESTED_INCLUDE_TEAMS=true

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Security alerts  [security]
#
# Open Dependabot and code-scanning alerts across the organisation, plus
# outstanding Dependabot pull requests.
# Alert counts alone are noise. This separates first-party findings from
# vendored dependencies so the critical ones are actually visible. Needs a
# token that can read security alerts.
# ARGUS_RULE_SECURITY_ENABLED=true
#
# Regular expression matching dependency paths to discount, e.g. vendored
# code. Matching alerts still appear in totals but not in the
# per-repository critical rollup.
# ARGUS_RULE_SECURITY_NOISE_PATTERN=\.venv|site-packages|node_modules|vendor/
#
# Repositories to leave out of alert counts, comma separated. Useful for
# archives, sandboxes or template repositories whose alerts you will never
# act on. This is in addition to ARGUS_EXCLUDE_REPOS, which applies
# everywhere.
# ARGUS_RULE_SECURITY_EXCLUDE_REPOS=
#
# Include code-scanning alerts. Turn off if the organisation does not use
# them.
# ARGUS_RULE_SECURITY_INCLUDE_CODE_SCANNING=true
#
# Include a count of open Dependabot pull requests.
# ARGUS_RULE_SECURITY_INCLUDE_DEPENDABOT_PRS=true

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stale branches  [stale_branches]
#
# Branches with no commit in a long time, excluding each repository's
# default branch.
# Abandoned branches accumulate quietly and make a repository harder to
# navigate. Yours are flagged so you can delete them.
# ARGUS_RULE_STALE_BRANCHES_ENABLED=true
#
# A branch with no commit in this many days counts as stale.
# ARGUS_RULE_STALE_BRANCHES_DAYS=60
#
# Only scan repositories pushed to within this many days. Keeps the sweep
# proportional to active work.
# ARGUS_RULE_STALE_BRANCHES_SCAN_DAYS=180
#
# Branch name prefixes to skip, comma separated.
# ARGUS_RULE_STALE_BRANCHES_IGNORE_PREFIXES=dependabot/

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stale pull requests  [stale_prs]
#
# Open pull requests older than the age threshold.
# Long-lived branches drift from main and get harder to merge the longer
# they sit. Either finish them or close them.
# ARGUS_RULE_STALE_PRS_ENABLED=true
#
# A pull request older than this many days counts as stale.
# ARGUS_RULE_STALE_PRS_DAYS=14
#
# Report bot-authored stale PRs as a single count rather than listing each
# one.
# ARGUS_RULE_STALE_PRS_COUNT_BOTS_SEPARATELY=true

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nobody has picked these up  [unreviewed]
#
# Open, review-ready pull requests with no reviewer and no team assigned.
# These are not blocked on any particular person, which is exactly why
# they stall. Someone has to volunteer.
# ARGUS_RULE_UNREVIEWED_ENABLED=true
#
# Skip draft pull requests, which are not asking for review yet.
# ARGUS_RULE_UNREVIEWED_EXCLUDE_DRAFTS=true
#
# Authors to ignore entirely, comma separated.
# ARGUS_RULE_UNREVIEWED_EXCLUDE_AUTHORS=app/dependabot
